LED matrix fixed display via /dev/fbchar

/dev/fbchar is a text device: reading lists supported patterns and usage;
writing displays one character or named pattern. One write accepts one character
from printable ASCII (32..126), or one pattern name:
  heart star smile sad up down left right check cross clear

Shell:
  cat /dev/fbchar  # finite list of supported patterns, then EOF
  echo A > /dev/fbchar
  echo 9 > /dev/fbchar
  echo heart > /dev/fbchar
  echo clear > /dev/fbchar

Python:
  fd = open("/dev/fbchar", 1)
  write(fd, "heart")
  close(fd)

This is a static single-glyph device. For text scrolling use `scroll`.
The existing /dev/fb accepts eight binary bytes instead; do not
confuse the two protocols. /dev/fbchar automatically enables scanning.
Output remains displayed after the process exits. Running multiple
framebuffer writers simultaneously may change the displayed pattern.
Unknown names and unsupported multi-character text are rejected.

Reading does not alter the display. Each open has an independent read
position; successive reads return EOF after the complete text.
